{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (via an automated cobbler/stitch pipeline) requested implementation of `cmd/users/main.go` for the go-unix-utils project. This is Task #5569 titled \"[stitch] Implement cmd/users core functionality (srd096 R1.1, R1.2, R1.3)\". The utility must read the utmpx database and print sorted, space-separated login names of currently logged-in users, matching GNU `gusers` behavior. The task requires creating a single file (`cmd/users/main.go`), following strict project conventions, and verifying compilation.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - utmpx database reading on Darwin/macOS (likely via cgo with `utmpx.h` C API or direct file parsing)\n   - SIGPIPE signal handling via `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Differential testing pattern against GNU reference binaries (gusers)\n   - Go module structure: `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils`\n   - Platform: Darwin (macOS) - `stat_darwin.go` build tag confirms Darwin-specific code\n   - Copyright headers: `// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.` + SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n   - No git commands allowed - orchestrator manages git externally\n   - Function size hard limit: 40 lines\n   - File size limit: 500 lines\n   - Write all files before building (no incremental compile-fix loops)\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd096-users.yaml` (READ)\n     - This is the SRD defining all requirements for the users utility\n     - R1.1: Read utmpx, print login names sorted alphabetically, space-separated, single line\n     - R1.2: Accept optional FILE argument for alternate utmpx file\n     - R1.3: Duplicate login names must appear once per session (NOT deduplicated)\n     - R2.1: Exit 0 on success\n     - R2.2: Exit 1 on error  \n     - R2.3: SIGPIPE handling via pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n     - Non-goals: No idle time, terminal, or remote host info (that's `who`)\n     - Package imports: `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys`\n\n   - `cmd/users/main.go` (TO CREATE)\n     - The main file to implement - does not exist yet\n     - Must implement all R1.x and R2.x requirements\n\n   - `pkg/sys/sigpipe.go` (PROVIDED IN CONTEXT)\n     - Contains `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` function\n     - Pattern: creates buffered channel, signal.Notify for SIGPIPE, goroutine exits with os.Exit(0)\n     ```go\n     func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() {\n         c := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n         signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE)\n         go func() {\n             <-c\n             os.Exit(0)\n         }()\n     }\n     ```\n\n   - Key existing patterns from provided source code showing project style (pkg/sys/, pkg/format/, pkg/testutils/, etc.) - all provided inline in the prompt\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet - only one tool call was made (Read of the SRD file) before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Read the SRD to understand full requirements before implementation\n   - Key design challenge identified: accessing utmpx on Darwin requires either cgo (to use `getutxent()` from `utmpx.h`) or direct parsing of the utmpx binary file\n   - The task specifies design decision D2: \"Use the utmpx C API via cgo or the Go syscall interface to read login records, matching GNU users behavior on Darwin\"\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - First message: Extremely detailed task prompt containing the entire project context, source code, SRDs, architecture, vision, roadmap, execution constitution, coding standards, go style guide, constraints, task description, requirements, design decisions, acceptance criteria, shared protocols, and package contracts. The core request is to implement `cmd/users/main.go` following specific requirements